Found while implementing encrypt_in_place (karr #4).

encrypt_in_place and edit now write through File::SOPS::_replace_file: a temporary
file in the same directory, then rename. A failure anywhere before the rename
leaves the original exactly as it was. The two older write paths still do not:

- encrypt_file with `output` omitted defaults to writing over its INPUT, and it
  does so by opening it with `>:raw` -- the plaintext is truncated the moment the
  handle is opened. This is the same hazard encrypt_in_place was built to close,
  reachable through the older method.
- rotate writes back to the same file the same way. A rotation interrupted at
  that point leaves a truncated encrypted file whose data key was already
  replaced, so the previous content is unrecoverable.

The fix is one line each -- _replace_file($path, $content) -- and the helper
already handles the case both need (the target exists, so its permissions are
copied onto the replacement).

Two consequences to write into the POD when it is done, because they are what
makes this a decision rather than a cleanup: the file gets a NEW INODE, so hard
links to it keep the old content, and _replace_file resolves a symlink so the
target is replaced rather than the link. sops itself truncates and rewrites,
keeping the inode; this distribution trades that for not destroying the file.

Deliberately left out of #4 to keep that change reviewable.
Scope decisions, both open questions in the ticket, decided rather than assumed:

1. encrypt_file with an EXPLICIT output writes atomically too, not only the
   defaulted in-place case. The hazard is not confined to the default: open ">"
   truncates an existing output before the first byte and neither the print nor
   the close was checked, so a short write left an empty file AND returned 1.
   Branching on "is output the same file as input" would be a path-comparison
   problem (x.yaml vs ./x.yaml vs a symlink to it) that silently falls back to
   the unsafe path when it guesses wrong. One write path also means one set of
   consequences to document and for a caller to remember.

2. decrypt_file HAS the same defect and is fixed with it, though the ticket did
   not name it. output being required removes the "defaults to the input"
   hazard, not the "output names a file that matters" one -- a working copy
   being refreshed, or the encrypted input itself. It writes plaintext, so a
   silently truncated result is the worse half of the bug, and the fix is the
   same line.

3. Permissions deliberately NOT changed. _replace_file now applies 0666 & ~umask
   when the target does not exist, because File::Temp creates 0600 and routing
   these three methods through the helper would otherwise have smuggled a
   permissions policy change into a data-loss fix. Measured (sops 3.13.3, umask
   022): --output creates 0644 and leaves an existing file mode alone; we match
   both. Whether decrypt_file SHOULD create 0600 is karr #45.

4. Added: a target that exists and is NOT a regular file (/dev/stdout,
   /dev/null, a fifo) is written through directly instead of temp+rename. Not
   in the ticket, but required by it: File::Temp cannot create a temp in /dev,
   so without this the fix would have broken anyone using output => /dev/stdout
   -- which sops supports (measured: sops -e --output /dev/stdout works).

Measured against sops 3.13.3 before choosing any of it:
  -e -i            keeps the inode AND the mode (hard links follow along)
  --output NEW     0644 under umask 022
  --output EXISTS  inode and mode preserved
  -e -i symlink    link left alone, target rewritten
  -e -i 0444 file  REFUSES ("Could not open in-place file for writing") -> #46

